WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.160
Let's say you're trying to lose 20 pounds,
or boost your immunity,

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.680
or increase your ability to fight COVID,
or even cancer.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.840
Well, the amazing thing is,
with the right diet,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.040
you're well on your way to
achieving these vital health goals.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.480
Welcome to the Nutrition Facts Podcast.
I'm your host, Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.880
Today, it’s all 3MCPD
– all the time.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.480
And if you’re wondering what
in the world I’m talking about,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.080
all you need to know is one word
– chlorohydrin.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.960
It’s a compound found in items like
Bragg’s Liquid Aminos and soy sauce.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.080
But is it safe? Let’s find out.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.760
In 1978, chlorohydrins were
found in protein hydrolysates.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.080
What does that mean?

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.360
Proteins can be broken
down into amino acids

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.840
using a chemical process
called hydrolysis,

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.320
and free amino acids like glutamate
can have taste-enhancing qualities.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.880
That's how they make cheap soy sauce
and seasonings like Bragg's liquid aminos.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.480
This process requires high heat,
high pressure, and hydrochloric acid

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:07.760
to break apart the protein.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.120
The problem is that when any residual
fat is exposed to these conditions,

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.240
it can form toxic compounds
called chlorohydrins.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.280
But when I say toxic, we're talking
about toxic to mice and rats.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.320
Chlorohydrins like 3-MCPD are
considered a worldwide problem

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:27.360
of food chemistry,

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.040
but no clinical studies on humans
have been reported so far.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.000
The concern is about detrimental
effects on kidneys and fertility.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.840
In fact, there was a time in which
it was considered as a potential

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.840
male contraceptive because it
could so affect sperm production.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.160
However, research funding
was withdrawn

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.400
after unacceptable side-effects
were observed in primates.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.160
They found flaccid testes in rats,
which is what they were going for,

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.120
but it caused neurological
scars in monkeys.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.680
What do you do, though, when
there are no studies in humans?

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.920
How do you set some kind of
safety factor? Well, it's not easy.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.640
What you do is take the lowest-observed-
adverse-effect level in animal studies,

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.200
which in this case was kidney damage,
then add in some kind of fudge factor,

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.320
and arrive at an estimated
tolerable daily intake,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.760
which for 3-MCPD means
that high level consumers

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.000
of soy sauce may
exceed the limit.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.800
But this was based on extraordinarily
high contamination levels.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.520
Since then, Europe introduced a
regulatory limit of 20 parts per billion

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.400
of 3-MCPD in hydrolyzed
vegetable protein products

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.160
like liquid aminos and soy sauce.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.360
The U.S. standards are much laxer,
though, allowing 50 times more:

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.120
1,000 parts per billion.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.080
I called Bragg's to see where they fell,
and the good news is that they are doing

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.680
independent third-party analysis
of their liquid aminos for 3-MCPD.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.360
The bad news is that despite my
pleas that they be fully transparent,

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.080
they wouldn't let me share
the results with you.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.560
I've seen them though, but I'm only
allowed to confirm they comfortably

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.800
meet the U.S. standards, but
fail the European standards.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.400
This is just the start
of the 3-MCPD story.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.800
If you test people's urine
for 3-MCPD or its metabolites,

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.080
100% of people turn up positive,
confirming that it's

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.480
a widespread food contaminant.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.560
I mean, 100% of people aren't downing
soy sauce or liquid aminos every day.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:37.040
But remember, the chemical resulted from
a reaction with residual vegetable oil.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.360
When vegetable oil itself is refined,
when it's deodorized and bleached,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.640
those conditions also lead
to the formation of 3-MCPD.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.680
And indeed, we've known for years
that various foods are contaminated.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.880
In what kind of foods have these kinds
of chemicals been detected?

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.800
Well, if it's in the oils and fats,
then it's in the greasy foods

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.600
made from them: margarine,
baked goods, pastries,

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.880
deep-fried foods, and fatty snacks
like potato and corn chips,

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.800
as well as infant formula.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.560
Here's the FDA limit
for soy sauce, a thousand,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.960
but doughnuts can have more
than 1,200, salami more than1,500,

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.760
ham nearly 3,000, and
French fries in excess of 6,000.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.080
So most people don't have
to worry about this problem

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.480
unless you're a consumer
of fried food. For example,

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.880
someone weighing about 150 pounds
who eats 116 grams of doughnuts

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.840
would exceed the maximum
tolerable daily intake,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.640
even if that was their
only source of exposure.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:40.720
Now, that's about two doughnuts,
but the same limit-blowing amount

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.120
of 3-MCPD could be found
in only five French fries.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:50.880
In our final story, we learn about
another reason to avoid palm oil

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.000
and we question the authenticity
of extra-virgin olive oil.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.160
Palm oil is the most commonly used
vegetable oil in the world today.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.240
Pick up any package of processed
junk in a box, bag, bottle, or jar,

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.480
and odds are it will have palm oil.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.840
Not only does it contain the primary
cholesterol-raising saturated fat

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.920
found mostly in meat and dairy, concerns
have been raised about the safety of

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:18.000
palm oil, given the finding that it
may contain a potentially toxic

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.640
chemical contaminant known as
3-monochloropropane-1, 2-diol,

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:25.680
otherwise known as 3-MCPD,
which is formed during

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.280
the heat treatment involved in
the refining of vegetable oils.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.600
So these contaminants
end up being widespread

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.280
in refined vegetable oils and fats,

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.440
and any products that contain them,
including infant formulas.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.280
It's been found in all refined vegetable
oils, but some are worse than others.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.520
The lowest levels of the toxic
contaminants were found in canola oil,

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.760
and the highest levels in palm oil.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:51.440
Based on the available data, this
may result in a signidicant amount

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.000
of human exposure, especially when used
to deep fry salty foods like French fries.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.360
In fact, just five fries could blow
through the tolerable daily intake.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.640
Now, if you just do this once in
a while, it shouldn't be a problem,

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.760
but if you're eating fries every day
or so, this could definitely be

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:11.440
a health concern. Because
the daily upper limit is based

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:15.040
on body weight, particularly high
exposure values were calculated

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:18.400
for infants who were on formula
rather than breast milk,

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.000
since formula is made from refined oils,
which—according to the European

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.040
Food Safety Authority—
may present a health risk.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.640
And estimated U.S. infant exposures
may be three to four times worse.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.120
If infants don't get breast milk,

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.760
there's basically no alternative to
industrially produced infant formula.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:41.280
Given that fact, the vegetable oil
industry needs to find a way

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.400
to reduce the levels of these
contaminants, and in the meanwhile,

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.240
this is yet another reason
that breast is always best.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.240
What can adults do to avoid exposure?

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.920
Well, if these chemicals are created
in the refining process of oils,

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.320
what about sticking to unrefined oils?

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:02.960
Refined oils have up to 32 times
the 3-MCPD compared to

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:04.800
their nonrefined counterparts,

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:08.000
with the exception
of toasted sesame oil.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.280
Sesame oil is unrefined—they
just squeeze the seeds—

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.920
but because they are
squeezing toasted sesame,

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:17.680
the 3-MCPD may have
come pre-formed.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:22.160
Virgin oils are by definition unrefined.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:27.040
They haven't been deodorized---the process
by which most of the 3-MCPD is formed.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:29.600
In fact, that's how you can
discriminate between the various

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:34.080
processing grades of olive oil.
If your so-called extra virgin olive oil

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:39.440
contains MCPD, then it must have been
diluted with some refined olive oil.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:44.560
The ease of adulterating extra virgin
olive oil, the dificulty of detection,

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:47.200
the economic drivers, and
the lack of control measures

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.840
contribute to the susceptibility
of extra virgin olive oil to fraud.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:53.920
How widespread a problem is it?

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.720
Of the 88 bottles off store
shelves tested, labeled

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:02.160
extra virgin olive oil, only 33
were found to be authentic.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.320
Okay, but what if you stick to
the top-selling imported brands

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:11.600
of extra virgin olive oil? Seventy-three
percent of those samples failed.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:14.560
Only about one in four
appeared to be genuine,

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.104
and not a single brand had even
half their samples pass the test.

